WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.362
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.547
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit eighteen: Phenomenology. Today you will describe experience using 'from a ... perspective' structures.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.481
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.515
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.502
Phenomenology.

7
00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:31.210
Phenomenology means the study of conscious experience as it appears to the person having it.

8
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:34.699
Phenomenology begins with description, not theory.

9
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:38.272
Your turn. Say: Phenomenology.

10
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.378
Phenomenology.

11
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:44.362
Lived experience.

12
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.660
Lived experience means the first-hand way something is actually felt and undergone.

13
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.863
Policy often ignores the lived experience of those it affects.

14
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.752
Your turn. Say: Lived experience.

15
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.634
Lived experience.

16
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.744
Intentionality.

17
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.537
Intentionality means the directedness of consciousness toward an object.

18
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.415
Intentionality means every thought is a thought of something.

19
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:17.006
Your turn. Say: Intentionality.

20
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:21.379
Intentionality.

21
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:23.106
Bracketing.

22
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:28.740
Bracketing means setting aside assumptions in order to describe experience as it is.

23
00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.522
Bracketing asks us to suspend our theories temporarily.

24
00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:35.525
Your turn. Say: Bracketing.

25
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.240
Bracketing.

26
00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:40.903
Perception.

27
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:45.275
Perception means the way something is sensed and interpreted.

28
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:48.730
Merleau-Ponty argued that perception is embodied.

29
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:51.899
Your turn. Say: Perception.

30
00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:55.758
Perception.

31
00:01:56.517 --> 00:01:58.470
First-person perspective.

32
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:04.188
First-person perspective means the point of view of the one who undergoes the experience.

33
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:09.024
Pain is known from a first-person perspective in a way no scan captures.

34
00:02:09.617 --> 00:02:13.035
Your turn. Say: First-person perspective.

35
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.201
First-person perspective.

36
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.079
Consciousness.

37
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:25.800
Consciousness means the state of being aware of oneself and one's surroundings.

38
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:30.322
For phenomenologists, consciousness is always consciousness of something.

39
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.848
Your turn. Say: Consciousness.

40
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.084
Consciousness.

41
00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:39.944
Embodiment.

42
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.912
Embodiment means the fact that experience is shaped by having a living body.

43
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:48.631
Embodiment shapes how we perceive space and effort.

44
00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:51.730
Your turn. Say: Embodiment.

45
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:55.719
Embodiment.

46
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.080
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:06.030
Visal, how does phenomenology differ from psychology? Both study experience.

48
00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:18.009
Phenomenology studies experience from a first-person perspective, describing how things appear, rather than measuring them from outside.

49
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.838
But isn't that unscientific?

50
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:31.325
From a methodological perspective it is rigorous. Bracketing asks us to set aside assumptions so that description comes first.

51
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:33.754
And what does it reveal?

52
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:44.677
From the perspective of embodiment, perception is not passive. We experience a hill as steep partly because of our own tired legs.

53
00:03:45.833 --> 00:03:47.554
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:52.227
Grammar focus: From a [X] perspective structures.

55
00:03:52.527 --> 00:03:53.784
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:54.483 --> 00:03:58.410
Phenomenology studies experience from a first-person perspective.

57
00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:02.756
From a methodological perspective, bracketing is rigorous.

58
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.229
From the perspective of embodiment, perception is never passive.

59
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:16.949
'From a … perspective' introduces the viewpoint: from a legal / historical / first-person perspective. 'Perspective' is singular after 'a'; it can start or end the sentence.

60
00:04:17.683 --> 00:04:22.960
Be careful! Don't say: In a first-person perspective, experience is studied.

61
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.386
Say: From a first-person perspective, experience is studied.

62
00:04:28.686 --> 00:04:30.337
Use 'from a … perspective'.

63
00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:33.623
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:36.444
Back to the Things Themselves.

65
00:04:36.744 --> 00:04:45.466
Edmund Husserl founded phenomenology with the call to return to the things themselves, that is, to describe experience as it is lived rather than as theories say it should be.

66
00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:52.332
Central to the method is bracketing, the temporary suspension of assumptions about whether the world is as it appears.

67
00:04:53.333 --> 00:05:00.894
Phenomenologists emphasise intentionality: consciousness is always directed toward something, whether a tree, a memory or a hope.

68
00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:11.225
Merleau-Ponty added that perception cannot be understood apart from the body; from the perspective of embodiment, we perceive the world through our ability to act in it.

69
00:05:12.233 --> 00:05:18.993
Phenomenology thus complements the sciences by describing what cannot be captured from a third-person perspective alone.

70
00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.904
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.745
Phenomenology studies experience blank a first-person perspective.

72
00:05:30.045 --> 00:05:32.932
A, in. B, from. C, at.

73
00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:38.153
The answer is B: from.

74
00:05:39.317 --> 00:05:42.837
Setting aside assumptions to describe experience is blank.

75
00:05:43.137 --> 00:05:47.336
A, bracketing. B, embodiment. C, intentionality.

76
00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.656
The answer is A: bracketing.

77
00:05:53.817 --> 00:05:57.012
The directedness of consciousness toward objects is blank.

78
00:05:57.312 --> 00:06:02.208
A, perception. B, intentionality. C, lived experience.

79
00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:08.207
The answer is B: intentionality.

80
00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:10.990
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:19.332
A, From a legal perspective, the claim is weak.. B, From legal perspective, claim weak.. C, From a legally perspective, the claim is weak..

82
00:06:22.867 --> 00:06:26.828
The answer is A: From a legal perspective, the claim is weak..

83
00:06:27.983 --> 00:06:32.433
According to the reading, phenomenology complements science by describing what…

84
00:06:32.733 --> 00:06:39.068
A, a third-person view cannot capture. B, scientists already know. C, cannot be experienced.

85
00:06:42.633 --> 00:06:46.316
The answer is A: a third-person view cannot capture.

86
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.640
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:51.483 --> 00:06:55.364
What does it mean to study experience from a 'first-person perspective'?

88
00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.674
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:13.974 --> 00:07:40.397
To study experience from a first-person perspective means describing it as it is lived by the person undergoing it, rather than measuring it from outside. A scan can show which area of the brain is active during pain, but only the first-person view tells us what the pain is like. Phenomenologists try to bracket their assumptions so that they describe rather than explain. The aim is faithful description.

90
00:07:40.697 --> 00:07:42.232
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:43.117 --> 00:07:48.056
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and from a [x] perspective structures.

92
00:07:48.356 --> 00:07:53.412
Today's value is reflective awareness. Notice your own experience with care and honesty.

93
00:07:53.712 --> 00:07:56.306
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:56.606 --> 00:07:59.177
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

